  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 252 PM CST Mon Nov 23 2026\n\nBy the middle of this week, attention turns to an increasing threat \nfor a rain/snow mix (mostly snow) from late Wednesday evening into \nThanksgiving. The synoptic setup associated with this system is \nunusual which is leading to a lower confidence forecast. While the \nparent low is well to the southeast across the Carolinas, it also \nfeatures a deepening closed low centered near our area over the\nMid-Mississippi Valley. The slow moving closed low aloft is \nexpected to result in frontogenesis somewhere near the region \nas it strengthens Wednesday night into Thanksgiving resulting \nin a frontogenetically forced band of precipitation.\n\nThe most uncertain part of this forecast is related to where exactly \nthis band sets up with possible locations ranging from the \nMississippi River to as far east as Indiana. Most guidance has \ntrended westward with this band since yesterday while a few members \ntook a jog back east, highlighting the uncertainty related to the \nlocation of this band.\n\nForecast soundings within this band reveal a well-saturated DGZ with \nsufficient lift centered near the DGZ indicating dendrites should be \nthe prominent ice crystal type. In addition, guidance has good \nconsensus in a sub-freezing layer from the DGZ to near the surface, \nwhich would preserve falling dendrites and possibly result in \naggregation where temperatures are between -5 and 0C. Surface \ntemperatures should be near the freezing mark within this band of \nprecipitation with a warming trend from sub-freezing temperatures \nWednesday night/Thanksgiving morning to above freezing into \nThanksgiving day. As a result, the most favored precipitation type \nwithin this band is expected to be a wet snow with a transition to \nlight rain/snow mix on the outskirts of the band (where dynamical \ncooling is less significant) and through the day Thursday when \nprecipitation rates taper off and surface temperatures warm further.\n\nThe mean accumulated precipitation on the LREF indicates a west to \neast gradient of values (higher to the east) with QPF values >0.5\" \nalong and to the east of the Mississippi River. Considering the \nforecasted atmospheric profile mentioned above, its reasonable to \nassume that SLR's should range from 7:1 - 10:1 (slightly below \nclimatology 12:1). Combining the mean QPF and forecast SLR's reveals \nthat localized snowfall totals in excess of 5\"+ would be possible \nwithin the mesoscale band, assuming all of the QPF falls as snow and \ndoesn't melt. Even with relatively warmer ground temperatures, \nmoderate snowfall rates would lead to accumulation on roadways and \npotential travel impacts, especially if any of this snow falls \nWednesday night/early on Thanksgiving. This is a low confidence \nforecast with a high variance of potential snowfall amounts for any \ngiven location. As a result, continue to stay tuned to the latest \ninformation as this will be a quickly evolving forecast.\n\nAny snow that ends up accumulating across the area Wednesday \nnight/Thanksgiving (if any) will start melting once precipitation \nends from west to east around Thanksgiving evening. Long-range \nguidance is in good agreement that 850mb temperatures warm back to \nabove 0C by Friday and near 5C into the weekend. This would result \nin afternoon highs back into the 50s by this weekend and is \nhighlighted by LREF temperature IQR spreads of 5-10 degrees centered \non values near climatological normals.",
